 "result": "In this repo (a Flask-style framework renamed `tessel`), the conversion and dispatch are in `src/tessel/views.py`:\n\n- **`View.to_view_func(name, ...)`** (classmethod, `views.py:86`) turns the class into a plain view function.\n  - The inner `view(**kwargs)` creates a `cls` instance, or reuses one if `init_every_request` is `False`.\n  - It then calls `self.run_view(**kwargs)` through `active_app.ensure_sync`.\n  - It copies `view_class`, `methods`, `provide_automatic_options`, `__name__`, `__doc__` and `__module__` onto the function.\n  - It applies any `decorators` before the function is registered with `app.attach_route_rule(..., view_func=...)`.\n- **`VerbView(View)`** (`views.py:138`) is the class that provides `get()`/`post()` routing.\n  - `VerbView.__init_subclass__` (`views.py:165`) builds `cls.methods` from the lowercase HTTP-method attributes the class defines (those in `http_method_funcs`), such as `{\"GET\", \"POST\"}`. The route registration uses this set as the allowed methods.\n  - `VerbView.run_view` (`views.py:182`) picks the handler with `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`. If the method is `HEAD` and no `head` handler exists, it falls back to `get`.",
